    "math_academy_id": "q-160026",
    "knowledge_point_id": "b346e7e1-b641-5a97-9677-84a0ff6ab2dc",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding Missing Values in a Data Set Given the Mean",
    "problem": "Given that the mean of the following data set is $10,$ find the value of $b:$ \n\n$$\nb,\\,1,\\,b,\\,1,\\,b\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=5$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i} & =b+1+b+1+b \\\\  & =2+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is given by the expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (2+3b) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+3b}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we're told that $\\overset{―}{x}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{2+3b}{5} \\\\ 50 & =2+3b \\\\ 48 & =3b \\\\ b & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
